node_modules/
.wrangler/
.dev.vars
.DS_Store
*.log

# public/index.html IS committed on purpose: the ledger data is meant to live in this
# repo ("the data lives here, at github"). It is a BUILD OUTPUT of the Cow-Crm01 master
# (see CLAUDE.md), so never hand-edit it; `npm run build` regenerates it and the daily
# run rebuilds and commits it. This repo is PRIVATE because that data is real.

# Never commit real names. The desk ships codes only; the plaintext directory and the
# encrypted vault live on the laptop, never here. These are a safety net.
salt_bio.json
salt_vault.json
*.vault

# THE STATEMENT PASSWORDS. One per customer, plaintext, in this one file and nowhere else, so
# he can read one back to a customer who has lost it, and so a retry of the same issue reuses
# them rather than invalidating every password already sent. They are credentials: this repo is private, but a
# credential committed is a credential in the history for ever, and these are re-issued monthly
# precisely so nothing has to live that long. The Worker never sees them either; it holds only
# a PBKDF2 verifier. The encrypted statements beside them (statements/*/_kv/) ARE committed on
# purpose: that is ciphertext, and it is what the deploy uploads to KV.
statements/*/_passwords.json
# THE SEND SHEET, for the same reason. It is the monthly send in one page, one card per customer
# with the message on a Share button and the password on a second, so it carries every password in
# the issue with a nice face on it. It also puts every account beside every other, which is the one
# thing a statement must never do. It is written beside the statements, read on the laptop, and
# never committed and never sent.
statements/*/_send_*.html
# THE KEY AND THE MASTER, on the laptop (03 Sep 2026). {"key": <STMT_KEY as hex>, "master": <the
# STMT_MASTER passphrase>}. The key derives every customer's content key, and the cloud holds it
# as a GitHub Actions secret; it is never in the tree. The publish scratch beside it is what the
# deploy hands to wrangler and is rewritten every run.
statements/_secrets.json
statements/.publish/

# Test scratch
shot-*.png
test/tmp/
# THE SUITE'S OWN SCRATCH UNDER test/, AS A CLASS (04 Sep 2026).
#
# Several blocks write a MUTATED COPY OF THE WHOLE BOOK and of the master into test/ under fixed
# dot-names, run the desk against it, and delete them at the end: .v408.*, .v410.*, .r7tmp.*,
# .v445.<tag>.*, and a .run.html the build leaves beside one of them. A run that dies before its
# cleanup line, or two suites running at once (which is exactly what an audit does), leaves them on
# disk. Nothing ignored them, and tools/update.mjs step 7 runs `git add -A`, so a mutated copy of
# the book was one interrupted test run away from being committed AS the book.
#
# IGNORED AS A CLASS AND NOT ONE BY ONE, because the names are the fault: each is minted by the
# fold that needed it (v408, v410, v445, round seven) and the next fold will mint another that
# nobody remembers to add here. Every dot-file directly under test/ is scratch by this repo's
# convention; a deliberate one would need `git add -f` and a negation beneath this line.
#
# This is the containment, not the repair: the blocks should write under test/tmp/ (already
# ignored) and clean up in a finally, so a dead run leaves nothing at all.
test/.*

# Local Claude Code settings, personal to this machine
.claude/settings.local.json
migrations/.seed.generated.sql
migrations/.query.sql

# Scratch: scoping notes and drafts, never part of the deploy.
.scratch/

# zero-byte leftovers from the ledger extract tooling
.scratch_v*.mjs

# THE FOLD'S OWN SCRATCH INPUT, and it must never be committed. master/_to_fold.json and
# master/_folded.json ARE tracked, because they are the handoff between the stage job, the
# agent and the deploy. _fold_notes.json is not part of that: it is the notes skeleton
# tools/fold.mjs writes for the agent to fill in, and it is spent the moment --apply reads it.
# A committed copy is worse than clutter. --plan prints "_fold_notes.json exists; --apply will
# use it" and leaves it alone, so a stale one left in the tree is a set of notes from a batch
# that is already folded, sitting where the next --apply will pick it up.
master/_fold_notes.json

# THE LAUNCHER PAGE IS GENERATED, AND TRACKING IT MADE EVERY WORKTREE DIRTY. _Open.html is
# the session-start index the launcher writes (LAUNCHER.md), and it stamps the FOLDER NAME
# into its title, its heading and its file count. In a git worktree that name is the
# worktree's, so it regenerates as "eloquent-galileo-382b2c: open files" rather than
# "salt-command" and shows as modified in every session before a line of work is done. That
# is noise a reader has to learn to ignore, and worse, `git add -A` sweeps it into whatever
# commit is being made: update.mjs step 7 does exactly that, so a chart fix would have
# carried a launcher page with a stranger's folder name in it.
# THE FILE STAYS ON DISK. _MasterOpen.html links to it and the launcher rewrites it on every
# open, so it is untracked here rather than deleted. Generated pages are never hand-edited
# and never versioned; this one was the exception and should not have been.
_Open.html
test/_render/
